landed: done (2026-07-10)
--------------------------

CORRECTION to this plan's central premise: "PURGE BEFORE RE-LOADING"
is empirically WRONG for Headless.Session's actual API, and actively
harmful if followed literally. Three experiments, in order:

  1. purge_theories(List(name), master_dir = "") (the naive call the
     plan implied): silently resolved to the WRONG Document.Node.Name
     (import_name bakes master_dir into the node's identity, and ""
     != the real master_dir), so it purged nothing -- yet the SECOND
     use_theories call still correctly reported the on-disk edit. That
     was the first sign the purge wasn't doing (or needing to do)
     anything.
  2. purge_theories(List(name), master_dir = <the real one>) (fixing
     the resolution bug): this time the purge actually matched the
     loaded node -- and the very next use_theories call produced a
     CORRUPTED document (duplicated theory headers, cascading "missing
     theory context" outer-syntax errors). Root cause: Headless.
     Resources.purge_theories only updates its own Scala-side
     bookkeeping (state.theories) and never calls session.update with
     the purge_edits that would tell the live prover document to
     actually drop the old text. So Resources ends up believing the
     node has no prior content, while the prover's actual document
     still holds the old text; the next use_theories diffs the new
     file against that phantom "empty" baseline and inserts the whole
     new theory on top of the still-present old one.
  3. no purge at all -- just call session.use_theories(...) again:
     clean, correct staleness detection, both directions (good ->
     bad -> good), no corruption. use_theories reads the file fresh
     off disk and diffs against its OWN correctly-tracked prior
     content (kept in sync by its own internal load_theories/
     update_theories bookkeeping) on every call, so a plain re-run
     already picks up on-disk edits -- no purge involved.

check_theory's implementation is now just `use_theories_result(name,
master_dir)`, unconditionally -- no purge step. The corrected finding
and the three experiments are recorded in mcp_session.scala's
check_theory comment and CHANGELOG (2026-07-10, "wave 2"). unload_
theory still needs a REAL document-level removal (that's its whole
job), so it uses a different mechanism -- Resources.clean_theories,
which does call session.update -- see plans/unload_theory's status
block.

T1 (bridge and e2e): the staleness three-beat -- load a fixture (ok),
edit it on disk to a failing proof, check_theory -> isError with a
line position for the NEW content (not the stale ok from the first
load), edit it back, check_theory -> ok again. T2 (bridge): check_
theory on a never-loaded fixture works (the purge-no-op path is now
moot -- there is no purge -- but the "works on a fresh theory" claim
still holds since use_theories handles first-load and re-load
identically). T3 (bridge): a sorry-only fixture is an ok result whose
text mentions "warning"; a fixture with a genuine failing proof is
isError -- confirming the pinned policy (isError only on genuine
errors, never on warnings alone). T4 (e2e, test_mcp.py, "wave 2:
flagship -- ..."): approximates the full load -> attach -> prove ->
repl_text -> splice -> check_theory chain -- a REPL proves a lemma and
repl_text extracts the script (repl_init_from_source, the "attach"
step, is still a deferred wave-1 tool, so the REPL proves independently
rather than attaching to the loaded theory's document), python splices
the script into the fixture file, check_theory confirms it ok on disk.

